-- url     : https://prove2.me/theorems/158f824e-0341-5654-b45a-dd265c6428f0
-- title:
--   Node place-sets avoid smooth residue discs, case q=2
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $q$ is a prime with $q = 2$, $M' \ge 1$ is an integer with $q \nmid M'$, and $\ell$ is a prime with $\ell \equiv 11 \pmod{12}$ and $\ell \mid M'$. Further, $A$ is a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ lying over $q$ in the sense of `LiesOverPrime`, i.e. $q$ is a non-unit of $A$; write $\kappa =$ `ResidueField A`. A place of a field extension $F/K$ is, as in `Place`, a valuation subring of $F$ containing the image of $K$, different from $F$ itself and a principal ideal ring; `P.ord` is the associated normalised integer valuation, `P.IsRational` says that $K \to$ `P.ResidueField` is surjective, and `P.evalAt f` is the element of $K$ representing the residue of $f$ when $f$ is integral at $P$ (and $0$ otherwise).
--
--   On the level-$M'$ side: $W$ is a finite set of places of `modularFunctionFieldC κ M'` (the $\kappa$-subfield of $\kappa((q))$ generated by `jqModC` and its $M'$-th expansion) whose members are, by `hW`, exactly the supersingular places `ssPlaces q M' κ`, that is the rational affine geometric places $w$ with `w.evalAt (jGeomGen κ M')` in `ssJSet q κ`; $s$ is one such place. The inclusion `hle` asserts `modularFunctionFieldBar M' ≤ fieldBar q M'`, where the first is the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-base change of the full level-$M'$ modular function field and the second is `xHFunctionFieldBar (q ^ 2 * M') (levelH q M')`. The datum $R_0$ is a `ConstantReduction` of $A$ from `modularFunctionFieldBar M'` to `modularFunctionFieldC κ M'`: a valuation subring $R_0.\mathrm{integers}$, a surjective residue homomorphism onto `modularFunctionFieldC κ M'` with kernel the maximal ideal, compatibility with $A$ on constants, the existence for each non-zero $f$ of a scalar $c$ with $c \cdot f$ integral and non-zero residue, and a place map preserving degrees and orders of functions. The hypothesis `hR₀` says that $R_0$ computes coefficientwise reduction: for every Laurent series $y$ over $A$ whose coefficientwise image in $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}((q))$ lies in `modularFunctionFieldBar M'`, that element lies in $R_0.\mathrm{integers}$ and its $R_0$-residue, read as a Laurent series over $\kappa$, is the coefficientwise residue of $y$. Finally $\pi \in \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ satisfies $\pi^{q^2-1} = q$ and $\pi \in A$.
--
--   On the full-level side: $F_{\mathrm{ss}}$ is a field over $\kappa$ and $R$ a `RegularProlongation` of $A$ from `fieldBar q M'` to $F_{\mathrm{ss}}$ (a valuation subring with surjective residue map onto $F_{\mathrm{ss}}$ with kernel the maximal ideal, inducing $A$ on constants, and with the scaling property above). Attached to it are: a finite set $N$ of places of $F_{\mathrm{ss}}$ over $\kappa$; for every place $Q$ of $F_{\mathrm{ss}}$ a subring `Sx Q` of `fieldBar q M'`, a ring homomorphism `φx Q : Polynomial A →+* Sx Q`, a character `χ₀x Q : Sx Q →+* κ`, and a set `Dx Q` of places of `fieldBar q M'` over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$. The hypothesis `h0` provides an element of $F_{\mathrm{ss}}$ transcendental over $\kappa$. The hypothesis `h1` says that $R$ lies over $s$: for $f$ in $R_0.\mathrm{integers}$ which is non-negative at every place of `modularFunctionFieldBar M'` at which the $j$-function $\bar j$ (the image of `jq` under `coeffEmb`) is non-negative, and whose $R_0$-residue lies in the valuation subring of $s$, the image of $f$ in `fieldBar q M'` lies in $R.\mathrm{integers}$ and its $R$-residue is the image under $\kappa \to F_{\mathrm{ss}}$ of `s.evalAt` of the $R_0$-residue of $f$. The hypothesis `h2` says $R.\mathrm{integers}$ is preserved by pullback along every level automorphism `levelAutBar q M' ζ γ` with $\zeta$ a primitive $q$-th root of unity in $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ (an element of `Idx q`) and $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$, and `hcard` says $\#N = q + 1$.
--
--   The hypothesis `hpkg` is the smooth-point package: for every place $Q \notin N$, thirteen clauses hold: `Sx Q` contains the image of $A$; `φx Q` is formally smooth and formally unramified; `φx Q` is the canonical inclusion on constants $\mathrm{C}(a)$, and $\chi_{0x}Q$ composed with it is the residue map of $A$; $\chi_{0x}Q$ kills `φx Q X`; for every $c$ in the maximal ideal of $A$ there is a unique section $\chi :$ `Sx Q` $\to A$ which is the identity on constants, reduces to $\chi_{0x}Q$, and sends `φx Q X` to $c$; every $f \in$ `Sx Q` lies in $R.\mathrm{integers}$ with $R$-residue integral at $Q$ and with residue at $Q$ the image of $\chi_{0x}Q(f)$; the $R$-residue of `φx Q X` has $Q$-order $1$; `Dx Q` consists exactly of the rational places $P$ at which every element of `Sx Q` is integral with value in $A$, the value having $A$-valuation $< 1$ precisely when $\chi_{0x}Q$ vanishes on it; each section $\chi$ as above is realised by a unique $P \in$ `Dx Q`; for $P \in$ `Dx Q` the valuation subring of $P$ is the set of ratios $g/h$ with $g,h \in$ `Sx Q` and $P.\mathrm{evalAt}\,h \ne 0$; a non-zero $f$ with order $0$ at every $P \in$ `Dx Q` becomes, after multiplication by a non-zero constant of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, a unit of `Sx Q`; and an $f \in R.\mathrm{integers}$ integral at every $P \in$ `Dx Q` lies in `Sx Q`. The hypothesis `hdisj` says the discs of distinct places off $N$ are disjoint, `hcusp` that every $P \in$ `Dx Q` with $Q \notin N$ satisfies $0 \le P.\mathrm{ord}(\bar j)$, and `heqv` that for every $\tau$ in the subgroup generated by the level automorphisms which preserves $R.\mathrm{integers}$, the induced residue automorphism `R.resAut τ` stabilises $N$ and carries `Dx Q` to `Dx (R.resAut τ • Q)` for $Q \notin N$, where `smulDisc` is the translation of a set of places by $\tau$. The hypothesis `hUniq` is the corresponding uniqueness statement: for $Q \notin N$, any quadruple $(S, \varphi, \chi_0, D)$ satisfying the same thirteen clauses has $D =$ `Dx Q`, $S =$ `Sx Q`, and $\chi_0$ agreeing with $\chi_{0x}Q$ on common elements.
--
--   The node data are: a family of fields `FIx x` over $\kappa$ indexed by places $x$ of $F_{\mathrm{ss}}$, regular prolongations `Rx x` of $A$ from `fieldBar q M'` to `FIx x`, and places `bx x` of `FIx x` over $\kappa$; an index type $\Lambda$ with subrings `C' l` of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ contained in $A$, each a domain and a discrete valuation ring, with elements `ϖ' l`, a base index $l_0$; coefficient rings `Wc l`, each a complete discrete valuation domain, with elements `πW l`; exponents `E : Λ → ℕ` and $E_0$; sets of places `S nd` of `fieldBar q M'`, subrings `𝒩 nd` and `𝒩₀ nd l` of `fieldBar q M'`, the latter local (`hloc`) and Noetherian (`hnoe`); and elements `cx nd`, `cy nd`, `cu nd` of `fieldBar q M'`.
--
--   The hypothesis `hnodes` is a conjunction of clauses concerning these data (summarised here group by group). First, arithmetic of the coefficient rings: the maximal ideal of `C' l` is cut out by $A$, i.e. $d \in$ `C' l` reduces to $0$ in $\kappa$ iff $d \in$ `ϖ' l ⬝ C' l`; `C' l₀ ≤ C' l` for all $l$; `ϖ' l₀ ≠ 0`; every element of $A$ is algebraic over `C' l₀`; each `πW l` is irreducible and $1 \le E\,l$; every $\tau$ in `A.inertiaSubgroupIn ℚ` with `A.tameCharacter π τ = 1` fixes `ϖ' l₀`; and $(\varpi'_{l_0})^{E_0} = v\,\pi^{w}$ in $A$ for some unit $v$ of $A$ and some $w \ge 1$. Second, for each $nd \in N$: `bx nd`, $nd$ and all $P \in$ `S nd` are rational; `𝒩 nd` consists of the elements integral for `Rx nd`, for $R$, and at every $P \in$ `S nd`, and such elements have values in $A$ at every $P \in$ `S nd`; the crossing relation `cx nd * cy nd` $= (\varpi'_{l_0})^{E_0}\,$`cu nd`; `cx nd` reduces to $0$ under `Rx nd` and its $R$-residue has $nd$-order $1$, while `cy nd` reduces to $0$ under $R$ and its `Rx nd`-residue has `bx nd`-order $1$; for $\tau$ in the inertia subgroup with trivial tame character, the arithmetic Galois action `arithmeticGalois (xHFunctionField (q ^ 2 * M') (levelH q M')) τ` stabilises `S nd` and fixes `cx nd` and `cy nd`; every element of `fieldBar q M'` is a ratio of elements of `𝒩₀ nd l` for some $l$, and is also a $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-linear combination of elements of `𝒩₀ nd l₀` divided by a non-zero element of `𝒩₀ nd l₀`; and for each $l$: `𝒩₀ nd l₀ ≤ 𝒩₀ nd l ≤ 𝒩 nd`, the set `S nd` is exactly the set of places at which `𝒩₀ nd l` is integral and all its non-units take values in the maximal ideal of $A$, `C' l` maps into `𝒩₀ nd l` and meets every coset modulo non-units, elements of `𝒩₀ nd l` are linearly independent in the sense that a `C' l`-linearly independent family of scalars can annihilate only the zero family, `cx nd` and `cy nd` lie in `𝒩₀ nd l` with `cu nd` a unit there, and there are a homomorphism $\sigma$ from `Wc l` to the adic completion of `𝒩₀ nd l` and a ring isomorphism $\iota$ of that completion with the crossing model `UVCrossingModel (Wc l) (πW l ^ E l)`, namely `MvPowerSeries (Fin 2) (Wc l)` modulo $(X_0X_1 - \pi_W^{E\,l})$, such that $\sigma$ sends `πW l` to the image of `ϖ' l`, $\iota \circ \sigma$ is the constant map `const`, every image of `C' l` lies in the image of $\sigma$, and the orders of functions are read off from the two coordinates: an element whose `Rx nd`-residue is non-zero of `bx nd`-order $n$ is a unit multiple of $V^n$ modulo the ideal generated by `const (πW l)` and $U$, and an element whose $R$-residue is non-zero of $nd$-order $n$ is a unit multiple of $U^n$ modulo the ideal generated by `const (πW l)` and $V$. Third, the place-sets `S nd`, $nd \in N$, are pairwise disjoint. Fourth, the place-sets lie over $s$: for $nd \in N$, $P \in$ `S nd` and $f$ as in `h1` whose $R_0$-residue is integral at $s$, and for $a \in A$ whose residue is `s.evalAt` of that $R_0$-residue, the value $P.\mathrm{evalAt}$ of the image of $f$ minus $a$ lies in the maximal ideal of $A$. Fifth, equivariance: for every $\zeta'$ and $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ there is a permutation $\tau_N$ of places preserving $N$ with `levelAutBar q M' ζ' γ • P ∈ S nd` iff `P ∈ S (τN nd)` and with the pullback of `(Rx nd).integers` equal to `(Rx (τN nd)).integers`; and for every $\tau$ in the subgroup generated by the level automorphisms preserving $R.\mathrm{integers}$, `R.resAut τ • nd ∈ N` and `smulDisc τ (S nd) = S (R.resAut τ • nd)` for $nd \in N$. Sixth, compatibility with the level-$M'$ reduction: images of $R_0.\mathrm{integers}$ lie in `(Rx nd).integers`, and there is a ring homomorphism $j$ from `modularFunctionFieldC κ M'` to `FIx nd` compatible with the residue maps of $R_0$ and `Rx nd` and such that $g$ is integral at $s$ precisely when $j(g)$ is integral at `bx nd`.
--
--   The final hypothesis `hSEP1` is a separation statement: for every $x \in N$ and every place $y \notin N$ there is $g \in$ `fieldBar q M'` lying in $R.\mathrm{integers}$ and in `𝒩₀ x l₀`, a non-unit of `𝒩₀ x l₀`, whose $R$-residue is non-zero of $y$-order $0$, and which is integral at every rational place $P$ of `fieldBar q M'` with $0 \le P.\mathrm{ord}(\bar j)$.
--
--   Under these hypotheses the conclusion is: for every $x \in N$, every place $Q \notin N$ and every place $P$ of `fieldBar q M'` over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, if $P \in$ `S x` then $P \notin$ `Dx Q`.
--
--   This is the $q = 2$ case (at an auxiliary level rigidified by a prime $\ell \equiv 11 \pmod{12}$ dividing $M'$) of the statement that the place-sets attached to the nodes of the supersingular fibre of the full-level modular curve are disjoint from the residue discs of its smooth points, in the analysis of the semistable reduction at $q$ used for level lowering. It feeds the construction of node annuli and of a covering by node presentations with crossing units at the supersingular component.
